      lecture: [
        { say: 'Some series change sign at every term: plus, minus, plus, minus. Today you get a test for exactly those series, and a bonus: a very simple bound on the error when you stop early.',
          show: { type: 'board', tex: String.raw`1-\tfrac12+\tfrac13-\tfrac14+\cdots` } },
        { say: 'Here is the alternating harmonic series. The top graph shows the terms, the bottom graph the partial sums. Watch as the terms are added one by one.', show: 1 },
        { say: 'See the zig-zag: each partial sum overshoots, and the next one comes back by a smaller amount. They close in on the horizontal line, the sum, which is the natural log of 2.' },
        { say: 'The purple band around the latest partial sum has a half-width equal to the size of the next term. The true sum always stays inside it. That band is the error bound of today\'s theorem.' },
        { say: 'Some notation. We write an alternating series as the sum of minus 1 to the n plus 1, times b n. The minus 1 factor only makes the signs; b n is the size of the n-th term.', clear: true,
          show: { type: 'board', title: 'Notation', tex: String.raw`\sum (-1)^{n+1}\,b_n,\qquad b_n=\text{size of term } n` } },
        { say: 'S N is the partial sum, the first N terms added up. S is the sum of the whole series. And R N, which is S minus S N, is the remainder: the error you make when you stop after N terms.',
          show: { type: 'board', tex: String.raw`R_N=S-S_N` } },
        { say: 'Here is the test, due to Leibniz. Three conditions: the sizes b n are positive, they decrease, at least from some point on, and they tend to zero.', show: 3 },
        { say: 'If all three hold, the series converges. Even better: the error after N terms is at most the next size, b N plus 1, and it has the same sign as the first term you left out.' },
        { say: 'Let us check the three conditions for the sum of minus 1 to the n plus 1, over n cubed. First strip off the sign factor: the size is b n equals 1 over n cubed.', clear: true, show: 4, step: 0 },
        { say: 'Condition 1: 1 over n cubed is positive, because 1 divided by a positive number is positive. So the signs really alternate.', step: 1 },
        { say: 'Condition 2, decreasing. n plus 1 is bigger than n, so its cube is bigger too.', step: 2 },
        { say: 'Same numerator 1, larger denominator: the fraction is smaller. So b n plus 1 is less than b n.', step: 3 },
        { say: 'Condition 3: as n grows, n cubed grows without bound, so 1 over n cubed goes to zero.', step: 4 },
        { say: 'All three conditions hold, so the series converges by the alternating series test.', step: 5 },
        { say: 'Now estimate its sum with the first four terms. Write them out: 1, minus one eighth, plus one twenty-seventh, minus one sixty-fourth.', clear: true, show: 5, step: 0 },
        { say: 'Convert each fraction to a decimal, to six places.', step: 1 },
        { say: 'Add them from left to right: S 4 is 0.896412.', step: 2 },
        { say: 'How big is the error? The theorem says: at most b N plus 1. To get it, put n equals N plus 1 into b n equals 1 over n cubed.', show: 6, step: 0,
          moves: { 'n-1': 'The n in b n is replaced by the bracket N plus 1, giving 1 over N plus 1, cubed.' } },
        { say: 'And the sign: the remainder has the sign of the first omitted term, which is minus 1 to the power N plus 1, plus 1.', step: 1 },
        { say: 'Now with numbers. We stopped after 4 terms, so N is 4. Put it into the bound.', show: 7, step: 0,
          moves: { 'n-1': 'The 4 slides into the bracket in place of N: 1 over 4 plus 1, cubed.' } },
        { say: '4 plus 1 is 5, and 5 cubed is 125. So the error is at most 1 over 125, which is 0.008.', step: 1 },
        { say: 'Which sign? The first omitted term has the sign minus 1 to the power 6. An even power of minus 1 is plus 1, so that term is positive.', step: 2 },
        { say: 'The remainder has that same sign, so it is positive: the true sum is bigger than S 4, but by at most 0.008.', step: 3 },
        { say: 'Put in S 4: the sum lies above 0.896412 and is at most 0.904412.', step: 4 },
        { say: 'Check: the true value, 0.901543, is inside that interval, just as the theorem promised.', step: 5 },
        { say: 'Your turn: the same steps for the sum of minus 1 to the n plus 1, over n times 3 to the n. One small step at a time; the lecture waits for you.', clear: true, show: 8 },
        { say: 'Well done. Same recipe: add the first three terms, the bound is the next size, 1 over 324, and the next term is negative, so the sum lies just below S 3.' },
        { say: 'A warning. All three conditions matter. Minus 1 to the n times n over n plus 1 alternates, but its sizes tend to 1, not to zero. So it diverges.', clear: true, show: 9 },
        { say: 'Quick check: you have computed S 6 of a series that passes the test. Where is the true sum?', show: 10 },
        { say: 'The first omitted term is the seventh one, with sign minus 1 to the 8, which is plus. So the remainder is positive and at most b 7: the sum lies above S 6, by at most b 7.' },
        { say: 'To sum up: check the three conditions. Then the error after N terms is at most the next size, and it has the sign of the next term.', clear: true,
          show: { type: 'board', title: 'Alternating series test', tex: String.raw`|R_N|\le b_{N+1},\qquad R_N\ \text{has the sign of the next term}` } },
      ],
